              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#QuantumPhaseSpaceAsJordanGeometry The quantum phase space as a Jordan-geometry given by a ringed topos
          %p
            Back when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            was discovered in the first half of the 20th century, it was eventually formalized as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            and the traditional modern formulation emerged, where, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            perspective,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            are represented by suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            and more generally by elements of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} of observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            are certain (namely positive and normalized) linear functionals on these
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states+on+star-algebras"}> states on star-algebras
            ).
          %p
            But one may still ask if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            in the definition of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            accurately capture the intended
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pascual+Jordan"} Pascual Jordan
            argued (
            %a{:href => "#Jordan32"}> Jordan 32
            ) that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            on the observables is more
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            than supported by the physics of states and observation, that instead only the underlying structure of what is now called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            should matter.
          %p
            Much later in 1978 this idea was formally validated by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfsen-Shultz+theorem"} Alfsen-Shultz theorem
            (
            %a{:href => "#AlfsenShultz78"}> Alfsen-Shultz 78
            ). This states that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            for given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            depends indeed only on the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            structure. This is not too surprising: the definition of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+on+an+operator+algebra"} state on an operator algebra
            does not even mention the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            structure but mentions only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+operator"} positivity
            structure, which is what the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            captures.
          %p
            Despite these insights,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebras"} Jordan algebras
            found and find only marginal attention in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            %a{:href => "#ASReview04"} review from 2004
            of the book of Alfsen and Shultz it says that back then Jordan algebras were hoped to shed light on conceptual problems of genuine
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            Much more recently in 2010, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Harding-D%C3%B6ring-Hamhalter+theorem"} Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem
            sheds a new light on the role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            structure. This theorem states mild conditions under which a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            structure on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            is equivalently encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
            of the full
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
          %p
            These commutative subalgebras themselves are of course of old fame in quantum mechanics, they are the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+contexts"}> classical contexts
            ” given by tuples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            that all commute with each other and hence which can all be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurement"} measured
            simultaneously without the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uncertainty+principle"} uncertainty principle
            interfering. These
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+contexts"} classical contexts
            played a crucial role in the discussion of the foundation of quantum mechanics in the first half of the 20th century: back then people argued that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            which do not commute with each other it is unclear what it means physically to form their sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A + B
            or their product
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            should be demanded to be a linear (and positive normed)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functional"} functional
            on all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} commutative subalgebras
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+contexts"} classical contexts
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-state"} quasi-state
          %p
            The issue of whether quasi-states are a more accurate description of quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurement"} measurement
            was settled in 1957 by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            (
            %a{:href => "#Gleason57"}> Gleason 57
            ), which says that given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            greater than 2, then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-states"} quasi-states
            are automatically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            also on the full non-commutative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-state"} quasi-state
            obsolete, but since that is a formally weaker notion the opposite attitude makes sense: what is traditionally taken as the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            is more accurately thought of as being a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-state"} quasi-state
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+contexts"} classical contexts
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
          %p
            Indeed, by combining the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfsen-Shultz+theorem"} Alfsen-Shultz theorem
            with the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Harding-D%C3%B6ring-Hamhalter+theorem"} Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            already when they have the same
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            only involves the commutative subalgebras themselves, this Jordan-Alfsen-Shultz-Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem crucially involves their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order"} order
            of inclusions, hence the actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            structure of their inclusions.
          %p
            Therefore there is an intrinsic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+theory"} order theoretic
            aspect in the standard foundations of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %em just
            order theory, for it is not the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            structure of inclusions alone that matters in the Jordan-Alfsen-Shultz-Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem, but that poset structure together with the actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+C%2A-algebra"} commutative C*-algebra
            structure of each
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+context"} classical context
          %p
            There is an elegant way to combine these two aspects: a system of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative algebras
            together with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order"} order
            of their inclusions is equivalently a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+object"} algebra object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            over the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            in hand this is a trivial statement, but in view of the above it is worthwhile to make explicit: the Jordan-Alfsen-Shultz-Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem (
            %a{:href => "#HardingDoering10"}> Harding-Döring 10
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Hamhalter11"} Hamhalter 11
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            is accurately formalized by a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+C%2A-algebra"} commutative C*-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            over a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
          %p
            It has been argued that this serves as a formalization of the views on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            that in the middle of the 20th century
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niels+Bohr"} Niels Bohr
            expressed in extensive but informal writing (see
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Scheibe73"} Scheibe 73
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            is, it must be expressible and must be expressed through classical contexts. In honor of this intuition, the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            deserve to be called
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+toposes"} Bohr toposes
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HeunenLandsmanSpitters09"} Heunen-Landsman-Spitters 09
          %p
            In fact, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            is fairly trivial as far as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            go, since, by the above, it is just a reflection – precisely: the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+reflection"}> localic reflection
            ” – of a purely
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+theory"} order theoretic structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            provides a home for the context-wise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+C%2A-algebra"} commutative C*-algebra
            structure which makes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            have the additional structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebraic
            datum on top of the purely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+theory"} order theoretic
            datum in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            structure of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
          %p
            Ringed toposes have of course a long tradition in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            (most famously in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            %a{:href => "#Hakim72"} thesis
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Monique+Hakim"} Monique Hakim
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            by the notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} higher ringed toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            below. In as far as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            are supposed to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} dual
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topos
          %p
            Notice that this is a different geometric interpretation of “quantum phase space” than the traditional idea that quantum phase space is an object in
            = succeed "!" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+geometry"} non-commutative geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            provides a natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
            interpretation of this, one might call it “Jordan geometry”.
          %p
            This geometric nature of the Bohr topos becomes more manifest as we consider its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality"} Gelfand duality
            this carries not an internal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+C%2A-algebra"} commutative C*-algebra
            but its
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+spectrum"} Gelfand spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+presheaf"} spectral presheaf
            (
            %a{:href => "#ButterfieldHamiltonIsham98"}> Butterfield-Hamilton-Isham 98
            ).
          %p
            While here we find this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+presheaf"} spectral presheaf
            as an accurate dual description of the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            based on the Jordan-Alfsen-Shultz-Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem, interest in the spectral presheaf originally came from the observation that it provides a clear geometric formulation of yet another theorem about the foundations of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kochen-Specker+theorem"} Kochen-Specker theorem
            (
            %a{:href => "#KochenSpecker67"}> Kochen-Specker 67
            ).
          %p
            This again amplifies the role of the “classical contexts” of commutative subalgebras: one may ask if there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+variable"} hidden variable
            description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            that allows to assign actual values to all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            such that in all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+contexts"} classical contexts
            this assignment behaves as an actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+observable"} classical observable
            in that it provides a (star-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} associative algebras
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+C%2A-algebra"} commutative C*-algebra
            of the classical context to the real numbers. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kochen-Specker+theorem"} Kochen-Specker theorem
            rules out such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+variable+theory"} hidden variable theory
            by stating that when the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            is that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operators"} bounded operators
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            greater than 2, then such a “hidden variable” assignment cannot exist.
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#IshamButterfield98"}> Butterfield-Isham 98
            ) it was observed, that this statement has a natural geometric interpretation in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+presheaf"} spectral presheaf
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"} global element
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            go. It behaves like a non-trivial space, and yet there is no way to map a point into it as a whole, maps of points into it exist only locally.
          %p
            In summary, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            incarnation of the Jordan-Alfsen-Shultz-Harding-Döring-Hamhalter characterization of quantum observables not only accurately captures the nature of quantum observables, but also makes other subtle nature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            becomes more explicitly evident than in other formulations.
          %p
            To see how one can get more out of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            incarnation of the quantum phase space, it serves to pass from plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            to the more general context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            demand that to a region of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            is to be assigned the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            as a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            structure that matters, hence the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            is naturally regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+toposes"} ringed toposes
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            of local observables to each local region of spacetime.
          %p
            Such “Bohrification of local nets of observables” were analyzed in (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten12"}> Nuiten 12
            ). There it was found that the natural structure of the transition functions of local nets of Bohr toposes of observables by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            automatically capture the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
            condition of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local quantum field theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#WoltersHalvorson13"} Wolters-Halvorson 13
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Nuiten12"} Nuiten 12
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            condition on spacetime nets of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+toposes"} Bohr toposes
            is equivalent to
            %em
              %a{:href => "local+net#StrongLocality"} strong locality
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "local+net#EinsteinLocality"} Einstein causality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            is the geometric incarnation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            structure on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ASReview04"} the AS review, 04
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pascual+Jordan"} Pascual Jordan's
            ideas from the 1930s to quantum field theory.
          %p
            Be that as it may, notice that generally local systems of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+toposes"} ringed toposes
            are to be expected to naturally encode
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            on general grounds: the modern
            = succeed "-style" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebras"} factorization algebras
            or similar assigns to subsets of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+critical+locus"} derived critical locus
            of local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            extremizing the given
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived space
            of solutions to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange equations
            of motion: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            (pre-quantum). In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+critical+locus"} derived critical locus
            is modeled explicitly as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
            it is in fact a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} higher ringed topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            in
            = succeed "-perspective" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} higher ringed topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            it remains a net of ringed toposes, sending ringed toposes incarnating classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+spaces"} covariant phase spaces
            to ringed toposes incarnating their quantized version as quantum phase spaces.
          %h2#RelationToTheNonCommutativePhaseSpace Relation to the traditional non-commutative geometry
          %p
            In the above we highlighted that by
            %a{:href => "#ListOfTheoremsInvoked"} the fundamental theorems
            on the foundations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            – going back to insights of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pascual+Jordan"} Pascual Jordan
            way back in 1930 and formally affirmed by more recent results by Alfsen-Shultz and Harding-Döring – it follows that, accurately speaking, quantum phase space is not really an object in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            %em non-associative
            “Jordan geometry” which is naturally captured by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            over the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
          %p
            This observation indeed puts doubt on the long and widely held believe that the quantum phase space is an object in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            in the first place.
          %p
            But that this is not really true was “well known” all along: it is pointed out for instance in the foundational text (
            %a{:href => "#BatesWeinstein97"}> Bates-Weinstein 97
            ) on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            represented by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \{-,-\})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            that carries
          %ol
            %li
              %p a commutative algebra of classical observables
            %li
              %p
                equipped with a compatible non-commutative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
                (the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
          %p
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of this data is to be thought of as applying to these two items separately:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a non-associative but commutative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
                structure of quantum observables is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
                of the commutative algebra structure of classical observables;
            %li
              %p
                a non-commutative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
                structure is the deformation quantization of the Poisson bracket
          %p
            and that if the quantization of both items is given by a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            structure, then the non-associative commutative Jordan algebra structure is the one induced by the anticommutator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mi B
                  %mo +
                  %mi B
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \circ B = \tfrac{1}{2}(A B + B A)
          %p while the non-commutative algebra structure is that given by the commutator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mi B
                  %mo −
                  %mi B
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [A,B] = \tfrac{1}{2}(A B - B A)
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Jordan+algebra#OriginInQuantumPhysics"} Jordan algebra – Origin in quantum physics
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan-Lie-Banach+algebra"} Jordan-Lie-Banach algebra
          %p
            To understand that this makes good sense notice that this decomposition is that into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematics"} kinematics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematics"}> kinematics
                — the construction of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
                and of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
                alone indeed does
                %em not
                involve the associative product of the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"}> dynamics
                — the commutator
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
                structure is used to impose quantum
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+flows"} Hamiltonian flows
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi exp
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp([A,-])
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
                generated by the observables
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            But then observe in addition that when we pass from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            axiomatized as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+automorphisms"} inner automorphisms
            of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp([A,-])
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            with infinitely many degrees of freedom (such as a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+automorphism"} inner automorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            is really an artifact of the degenerate case of finitely many degrees of freedom considered there.
          %p
            Instead, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            the
            = succeed "-evolution" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
            %a{:href => "#QuantumPhaseSpaceAsJordanGeometry"} above
            discussion, this is however, accurately speaking, not actually to be thought of as a net of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebras"} Jordan algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+toposes"} Bohr toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            structure remains.
          %p
            In this context it may be noteworthy to recall what is a well-kept secret: despite much work on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            with the traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            that demand to assign
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            to regions of spacetime, there is to date not a single non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field
            example in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            greater than 3 of these axioms. (There are plenty of interesting example in dimension 2, though, see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
            and pointers given there.)
          %p
            On the other hand, more recently the variant of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            axioms known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebras"} factorization algebras
            has been shown to admit plenty of interesting examples of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebra
            is indeed a net that assigns to a region of spacetime
            %em not
            an algebra structure. The algebra structure there is instead all encoded into the way that spacetime regions are included into each other.
          %h2#ListOfTheoremsInvoked List of theorems invoked
          %p
            For reference, the following lists those theorems about the standard foundations of quantum mechanics that are being referred to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#QuantumPhaseSpaceAsJordanGeometry"} above
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfsen-Shultz+theorem"} Alfsen-Shultz theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Harding-D%C3%B6ring-Hamhalter+theorem"} Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kochen-Specker+theorem"} Kochen-Specker theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nuiten%27s+lemma"} Nuiten's lemma
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretation of quantum mechanics
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+probability+theory"} quantum probability theory
                –
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+state"} classical state
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
                    %ul
                      %li
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+states+%28in+geometric+quantization%29"} space of states (in geometric quantization)
                      %li
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+on+a+star-algebra"}> state on a star-algebra
                          ,
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-state"} quasi-state
                      %li
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbit"}> qbit
                          ,
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell+state"} Bell state
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimer"}> dimer
                          ,
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+network+state"} tensor network state
                      %li
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state+preparation"} quantum state preparation
                      %li
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitude"}> probability amplitude
                          ,
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+fluctuation"} quantum fluctuation
                      %li
                        %p
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"} pure state
                        %ul
                          %li
                            %p
                              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+function"} wave function
                          %li
                            %p
                              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bra-ket"} bra-ket
                          %li
                            %p
                              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell+state"} Bell state
